Maxwell–Faraday in ARK form (long → short):

Classical testbed (long solenoid):
Inside a solenoid (radius R, turns per length n, current I):
B(t) = μ₀ n I(t)
Aφ(r,t) = ½ r B(t)
Eφ(r,t) = −½ r (dB/dt)

ARK bridges (one-time SI links):
Aφ = ΛA · kᶜ θᶜ
B²/(2 μ₀) = Λu · ½ kᶜ θᶜ²

Solve scalars directly from measured fields (point of use):
θᶜ = (ΛA / Λu) · (B² / μ₀) / Aφ
kᶜ = (Λu / ΛA²) · (Aφ² μ₀ / B²)

So at each r,t the causal slope θᶜ and stiffness kᶜ are not assumed, they are computed from the same Aφ, B you measure in the lab.

Now compute E with ARK definition:
E = −∂t A − ∇φ
In this symmetry ∇φ = 0, so
Eφ = −∂t (ΛA kᶜ θᶜ) = −∂t Aφ = −½ r (dB/dt)

Result:
That is exactly Faraday’s law in classical form. ARK did not tune anything: it derived θᶜ, kᶜ from measured Aφ and B, then the scalar update returned the same induced field.
